====================================================================================================
the extremal case the formula points at: a translated block {2m+1, ..., 2m+2k-1}
all elements of size ~ 2m, so N_d = 0 for every d < m and Q(0) is as large as it
can be relative to Gamma.  Predicted relative error ~ 1/(6k), the slowest decay
any family of distinct odd integers can produce.
====================================================================================================
      m    k      Gamma         Q(0)      sigma^2       pred   measured   ratio   k*pred
     12   12    26.9990        91.78       4031.0   0.022768  -3.688279 -161.997   0.2732
     18   18    39.0000       149.74      13606.5   0.011005  -0.995782 -90.487   0.1981
     24   24    51.0000       218.68      32254.0   0.006780  -0.466099 -68.745   0.1627
     30   30    63.0000       299.62      62997.5   0.004756  -0.265094 -55.738   0.1427
     36   36    75.0000       392.57     108861.0   0.003606  -0.131384 -36.433   0.1298

  k*pred settling means the error is Theta(1/k) for this family: slow, and still
  going to zero.  The formula therefore says the annealed prediction survives every
  family of distinct odd integers, with 1/k the worst rate available -- because
  Q(0) ~ m^2/6 and sigma^2 ~ k m^2, so the m cancels and only 1/k is left.
